{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_time_travel", "label": "Time travel panel", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_read_only_by_construction", "label": "Read-only by construction", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Nothing in this panel writes a journal, a snapshot store or a live actor. What did this look like is a question, and answering it must not change the answer."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_replay_fold", "label": "Replay fold registration", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Turning events back into state requires onEvent, which lives in your actor class and cannot be recovered from stored data. A registered fold works even when the actor is not running - a finished saga, a passivated entity, a journal inspected after the fact - and an explicit registration wins over auto-capture because it is a deliberate statement about how that id folds."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_auto_capture", "label": "Fold auto-capture", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "When the actor is running its own initialState and onEvent are borrowed. It can be switched off entirely if you would rather see raw events than a state derived from an onEvent you have not vetted."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_replaystate", "label": "replayState", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "State is reconstructed by the exact function PersistentActor recovery calls, including the snapshot fast-path and the two snapshot-integrity checks. A debugger running a slightly different replay would be free to disagree with what the actor actually recovers, which is precisely the thing you are using it to check. One consequence: travelling to sequence N uses the newest snapshot before N, so a jump into the distant past is as cheap as recovery."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_event_diff_view", "label": "Event diff view", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Shows the change between the selected point and the one before it, because what did this event do is usually the question rather than the absolute state; field paths are dotted and unchanged fields hide behind a toggle."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_event_page_cap", "label": "Event page cap and value truncation", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Pages are capped at 200 by default and 2 000 maximum so an unbounded read of a million-event journal cannot be requested, and large values are truncated for transport with the panel saying so - a debugger must not become the reason a system falls over."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_migration_envelope_inspection", "label": "Migration envelope inspection", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Where a migration envelope is present the manifest and schema version are shown, which is what you want when a migration is the thing under suspicion."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_time_travel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_read_only_by_construction", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_time_travel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_replay_fold", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_time_travel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_auto_capture", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_time_travel", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_devtools_time_travel_replaystate", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/devtools/time-travel.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"s